        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "Blueprinty is a small software company that makes tools for engineering firms that are preparing blueprint-based patent applications. The marketing team wants to make a strong claim: firms that use Blueprinty's software are more successful at getting patents approved. Ideally, we would test this by observing the same firms before and after they started using Blueprinty, or by randomly assigning some firms to use the software and others not to use it. That ideal data does not exist here. Instead, Blueprinty collected data on 1,500 mature engineering firms, including each firm's number of patents awarded over the last five years, regional location, age since incorporation, and whether the firm is currently a Blueprinty customer. This makes the question interesting, but also difficult. Blueprinty's customers were not randomly selected, so a simple comparison between customers and non-customers could mix together the effect of the software with other differences between the firms, such as age or region. The goal of this post is to use Poisson regression and maximum likelihood estimation to make a more careful comparison.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exploring the Data\n",
        "\n",
        "First, I load the data and create readable labels for the customer variable. The outcome variable is `patents`, which counts how many patents each firm received over the last five years."
      ],
      "id": "531d80d1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
