    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The customer and non-customer age distributions are broadly similar, but customers are slightly older on average. Age should still be controlled for because older firms may have more experience, more legal infrastructure, and more accumulated R&D capacity. The relationship may also be nonlinear: patent production could rise as firms mature, but eventually level off.\n",
        "\n",
        "## A Simple Poisson Model via Maximum Likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "The outcome variable is a count, so a Normal model is not a natural first choice. Patent counts cannot be negative, and they only take integer values such as 0, 1, 2, and 3. A Poisson distribution is a better starting point for this type of outcome because it is designed for non-negative count data.\n",
        "\n",
        "For one observation, the Poisson probability mass function is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f(Y_i \\mid \\lambda) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^{Y_i}}{Y_i!}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Assuming the observations are independent and identically distributed, the joint likelihood for all firms is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\lambda \\mid Y_1, \\ldots, Y_n) = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^{Y_i}}{Y_i!}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking logs turns the product into a sum:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\lambda) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[-\\lambda + Y_i\\log(\\lambda) - \\log(Y_i!)\\right]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The maximum likelihood estimate is the value of $\\lambda$ that makes the observed patent counts most likely."
      ],
      "id": "0d1979bd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: simple-log-likelihood-function\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "Y = blueprinty[\"patents\"].to_numpy()\n",
        "\n",
        "def poisson_loglikelihood(lam, Y):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Log-likelihood for iid Poisson data with one common lambda.\"\"\"\n",
        "    if lam <= 0:\n",
        "        return -np.inf\n",
        "    return np.sum(-lam + Y * np.log(lam) - gammaln(Y + 1))"
      ],
      "id": "simple-log-likelihood-function",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: log-likelihood-plot\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "lambda_grid = np.linspace(0.1, 8, 300)\n",
        "loglik_values = [poisson_loglikelihood(lam, Y) for lam in lambda_grid]\n",
        "\n",
        "best_lambda_grid = lambda_grid[np.argmax(loglik_values)]\n",
        "sample_mean = Y.mean()\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
        "plt.plot(lambda_grid, loglik_values, linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.axvline(sample_mean, linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=2, label=f\"Sample mean = {sample_mean:.3f}\")\n",
        "plt.axvline(best_lambda_grid, linestyle=\":\", linewidth=2, label=f\"Grid maximum = {best_lambda_grid:.3f}\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Log-likelihood for the simple Poisson model\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"lambda\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Log-likelihood\")\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "log-likelihood-plot",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The curve reaches its highest point close to the sample mean of patent counts. Visually, this means the best single Poisson rate for the whole dataset is the average number of patents per firm.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, we can see the same result by taking the derivative of the log-likelihood:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\lambda) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[-\\lambda + Y_i\\log(\\lambda) - \\log(Y_i!)\\right]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial \\ell(\\lambda)}{\\partial \\lambda}\n",
        "= \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left[-1 + \\frac{Y_i}{\\lambda}\\right]\n",
        "= -n + \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}Y_i}{\\lambda}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Setting this equal to zero gives:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-n + \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}Y_i}{\\lambda} = 0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat{\\lambda}_{MLE} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}Y_i = \\bar{Y}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This result makes intuitive sense: in a simple Poisson model with only one rate parameter, the best estimate of that rate is the observed average count."
      ],
      "id": "7a0f4b05"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: numeric-simple-mle\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "# Optimize over log(lambda), then transform back, so lambda is always positive.\n",
        "simple_result = minimize(\n",
        "    fun=lambda theta: -poisson_loglikelihood(np.exp(theta[0]), Y),\n",
        "    x0=np.array([np.log(Y.mean())]),\n",
        "    method=\"BFGS\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "simple_mle = np.exp(simple_result.x[0])\n",
        "\n",
        "simple_mle_table = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"Quantity\": [\"Numerical MLE\", \"Sample mean\"],\n",
        "    \"Value\": [simple_mle, sample_mean]\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "display(\n",
        "    simple_mle_table.style\n",
        "    .format({\"Value\": \"{:.6f}\"})\n",
        "    .set_caption(\"Simple Poisson MLE compared with the sample mean\")\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "numeric-simple-mle",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The numerical MLE and the sample mean are essentially identical. This is exactly what the derivative showed: when every firm is assumed to share the same patent rate, the maximum likelihood estimate of that common rate is the average number of patents in the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Poisson Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "The simple model assumes every firm has the same patent rate, which is too restrictive. A more useful model allows the expected patent count to vary by firm characteristics:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y_i \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\lambda_i)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\lambda_i = \\exp(X_i'\\beta)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The exponential function is important because $X_i'\\beta$ can be any real number, but $\\lambda_i$ must be positive. In this model, each firm's expected patent count depends on its age, age squared, region, and Blueprinty customer status.\n",
        "\n",
        "I include age squared because the effect of firm age is unlikely to be perfectly linear. I include region indicators because regional differences could affect patent output. One region must be dropped as the reference category to avoid perfect collinearity with the intercept. Here, Northeast is the reference region."
      ],
      "id": "9e811abf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: poisson-regression-data\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"intercept\": 1.0,\n",
        "    \"age\": blueprinty[\"age\"],\n",
        "    \"age_sq\": blueprinty[\"age\"] ** 2,\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "for region in sorted(blueprinty[\"region\"].unique()):\n",
        "    if region != \"Northeast\":\n",
        "        X[f\"region_{region}\"] = (blueprinty[\"region\"] == region).astype(int)\n",
        "\n",
        "X[\"iscustomer\"] = blueprinty[\"iscustomer\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "X_matrix = X.to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
        "Y = blueprinty[\"patents\"].to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
        "terms = X.columns.tolist()\n",
        "\n",
        "X.head()"
      ],
      "id": "poisson-regression-data",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: poisson-regression-loglikelihood\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "def poisson_regression_loglikelihood(beta, Y, X):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Poisson regression log-likelihood with lambda_i = exp(X_i beta).\"\"\"\n",
        "    eta = X @ beta\n",
        "    lam = np.exp(np.clip(eta, -50, 50))\n",
        "    return np.sum(-lam + Y * eta - gammaln(Y + 1))"
      ],
      "id": "poisson-regression-loglikelihood",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: manual-poisson-mle\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "manual_result = minimize(\n",
        "    fun=lambda beta: -poisson_regression_loglikelihood(beta, Y, X_matrix),\n",
        "    x0=np.zeros(X_matrix.shape[1]),\n",
        "    method=\"BFGS\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "beta_hat = manual_result.x\n",
        "\n",
        "# Compute a numerical Hessian of the log-likelihood at the MLE.\n",
        "hessian = approx_hess(\n",
        "    beta_hat,\n",
        "    lambda beta: poisson_regression_loglikelihood(beta, Y, X_matrix)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "vcov = -np.linalg.inv(hessian)\n",
        "manual_se = np.sqrt(np.diag(vcov))\n",
        "\n",
        "manual_results = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"term\": terms,\n",
        "    \"coefficient\": beta_hat,\n",
        "    \"std_error\": manual_se,\n",
        "    \"incidence_rate_ratio\": np.exp(beta_hat)\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "display(\n",
        "    manual_results.style\n",
        "    .format({\n",
        "        \"coefficient\": \"{:.4f}\",\n",
        "        \"std_error\": \"{:.4f}\",\n",
        "        \"incidence_rate_ratio\": \"{:.3f}\"\n",
        "    })\n",
        "    .set_caption(\"Manual Poisson regression MLE results\")\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "manual-poisson-mle",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The incidence rate ratio is `exp(coefficient)`. Values above 1 mean the expected patent count is higher, holding the other variables fixed. Values below 1 mean the expected patent count is lower.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Checking the manual MLE against `statsmodels`"
      ],
      "id": "999bc244"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: glm-check\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "glm_model = sm.GLM(Y, X, family=sm.families.Poisson()).fit()\n",
        "\n",
        "comparison = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"term\": terms,\n",
        "    \"manual_coef\": beta_hat,\n",
        "    \"glm_coef\": glm_model.params.to_numpy(),\n",
        "    \"manual_se\": manual_se,\n",
        "    \"glm_se\": glm_model.bse.to_numpy()\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "comparison[\"coef_difference\"] = comparison[\"manual_coef\"] - comparison[\"glm_coef\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "comparison_display = comparison.copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "display(\n",
        "    comparison_display.style\n",
